Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/ Here's a C++ function that uses the markdown library to convert markdown text to HTML:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdown.h>

/**
 * Converts markdown text to HTML.
 *
 * @param markdownText The markdown text to convert.
 * @return The converted HTML text.
 */
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    markdown::MarkdownIn markdown(markdownText);
    markdown::MarkdownOut htmlOut;

    markdown.render(&markdown, &htmlOut);

    return htmlOut.c_str();
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# This is a heading\n\nThis is a paragraph with **bold** text.";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);

    std::cout << htmlText << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This function uses the markdown library to convert markdown text to HTML. The `markdown::MarkdownIn` object is used to parse the markdown text, and the `markdown::MarkdownOut` object is used to generate the HTML output. The `render` function is used to perform the conversion.

Note: You need to install the markdown library to compile this code. You can install it using your package manager or by downloading it from the official website.

Also, this code assumes that the markdown text is well-formed and does not contain any invalid markdown syntax. If you need to handle invalid markdown syntax, you may need to add additional error checking and handling code.

